```

## 7.2 State transition requirements

| Current state | Event | Next state |
|---|---|---|
| BOOTSTRAP | Environment verified | READY |
| READY | Valid proposal received | PROPOSED |
| PROPOSED | Validation begins | VALIDATING |
| VALIDATING | Required validation passes | PREPARED |
| VALIDATING | Integrity failure | SUSPENDED |
| PREPARED | Commit quorum obtained | COMMITTED |
| COMMITTED | Execution authorized | EXECUTING |
| EXECUTING | Outputs completed | PERSISTING |
| PERSISTING | Checkpoint verified | CHECKPOINTED |
| Any active state | Integrity failure | SUSPENDED |
| SUSPENDED | Recovery requested | RECOVERY_PENDING |
| RECOVERY_PENDING | Recovery validation begins | RECOVERY_VALIDATING |
| RECOVERY_VALIDATING | Recovery certificate valid | RECOVERED |
| RECOVERED | New authorized execution | READY |

## 7.3 Suspension semantics

Suspension is a safety action.

When an integrity failure occurs:

1. Stop the affected workload.
2. Prevent dependent operations from committing.
3. Preserve the last verified committed state.
4. Record the incident.
5. Preserve available evidence.
6. Notify relevant validators.
7. Require an authorized recovery procedure.

Suspension should be scoped to the smallest independently safe execution boundary.

Unrelated workloads may continue only when their isolation and independence have been established.

