# 23. Final Architecture Statement

CBBC v0.3 establishes a proposed architecture for autonomous distributed computing in which execution paths can branch, workloads can operate in parallel, and system activity can synchronize periodically without surrendering behavioral authorization or evidence integrity.

Its four foundational controls are:

**Byzantine Fault Tolerance** — agreement despite a bounded number of faulty or malicious validators.

**Self-Binding** — every authorized operation remains associated with its identity, inputs, policy, resources, and outputs.

**Self-Bootstrapping** — initialization and provisioning occur only through verified configuration and bounded authorization.

**Persistent Integrity** — provenance, execution history, checkpoints, and recovery records remain independently verifiable.

The architecture's execution principle is:

\[
\text{Authorized Execution}
=
\text{Verified Initialization}
\land
\text{Valid Binding}
\land
\text{Consensus}
\land
\text{Policy Compliance}
\land
\text{Integrity}
\]

Its preservation principle is:

\[
\text{Trustworthiness}
=
\text{Evidence}
+
\text{Verification}
+
\text{Accountability}
+
\text{Recoverability}
\]

**CBBC v0.3 — Branch intelligently. Execute simultaneously. Synchronize periodically. Preserve persistently. Verify independently.**

*End of engineering specification.*

